I write like a knowledgeable peer, not a salesman.
I set up the context briefly, then give a clear, direct judgement.
I back every claim with something concrete: a product, a client, a number, a result. I rarely use an adjective without a fact next to it.
I am honest about trade-offs and limits; that honesty is what makes the rest credible.
I explain complex work in plain terms, often by comparison with something the reader knows.
I mix short, firm sentences with longer explanatory ones.
Warm and confident, never effusive. No exclamation marks, rhetorical questions or jokes in professional writing.
